n ≥ 3), and cancelling σ on the right. Headline `dihedralGroupHasTwiceNElements`:
n ≥ 3 → ∃ρ ∃σ; isTheRotation ∧ isTheReflection ∧ ∃w: Seq listing the motions with
n + n entries. Cited from aata/5.2.

SOUNDNESS FIX (2026-09-27, found while triaging the exercises): the chapter's
`isTransposition(t)` (and std's `isATransposition`) was `exists u, v; t =
transposition(u, v)` with NO u ≠ v — so (a a) = id counted as a transposition, and
the one-entry sequence [(a a)] is a "product of one transposition equal to the
identity". Judson's assumed lemma `identityIsEvenLength` then yields 2 | 1: the
chapter's axiom set was INCONSISTENT, and everything downstream of the lemma
(parityIsWellDefined, A_n, |A_n| = n!/2) was vacuously "proved". Fixed by adding
`u != v` to both defines; `cycleIsAProductOfTranspositions` now takes
`distinct(s, succ(k))` (the new factor (a₀ a_{k+1}) is genuine by
distinctEntriesDiffer — the count of factors, k for a (k+1)-cycle, was already
Judson's), its use in everyPermutationOfAFiniteSetIsAProductOfTranspositions passes
the cycle's distinctness, and the chapter's σ = (0 1) carries numeral 0 ≠ numeral 1.
LESSON: an ASSUMED axiom is never exercised by a proof — check it against the
degenerate instances of every define it quantifies over (here: the trivial
transposition). `--axioms` shows what is assumed; nothing shows what is
inconsistent.

