## Open, smaller

### Clause variables in a definition block take the FIRST PARAM'S SORT

Found while surveying the corpus for definition migrations (`7dff5a4`). A free
variable a definition's declaration does not bind takes the sort of the first
param. Right by luck for recursive clauses (`k` in `add(succ(k), b)` is a `Nat`
like `a`), wrong for the membership style:

    func union(a: Set, b: Set) => Set:
      member(x, union(a, b)) iff (member(x, a) or member(x, b))
    -- error: expected sort 'Set', got 'Element'   (at `x`)

The fix is to take the sort from USE (`member`'s first argument pins
`x : Element`), which subsumes the first-param heuristic. Blocks TIER 2 of
DEFINITION-MIGRATION-CANDIDATES. Hit again 2026-09-26 writing `cycle`:

    func cycle(s: Points, k: Nat) => Fn:
      cycle(s, ZERO) = identityFn;
      cycle(s, succ(k)) = compose(transposition(at(s, ZERO), at(s, k)), cycle(s, k))
    -- error: expected sort 'Nat', got 'Points'   (at `k`)

### A bare constant on a clause's RIGHT-HAND side is read as a clause variable

Same probe, first clause: `cycle(s, ZERO) = identityFn` is rejected with
"'identityFn' shadows a declaration; choose a fresh name" — the RHS constant is
taken for a fresh clause variable. (`ZERO` in the HEAD is accepted as a pattern.)
Resolution should look identifiers up before minting clause variables. Until
both are fixed, `std/permutation/cycle.b4m` states its two clauses as plain
axioms (`cycleZero`, `cycleSucc`).

